{"node_id":"NRS_484B_280_1a","law_refs":["NRS-484B-280-1a"],"subject":{"binder":"ego","class_elements":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-130::motor_vehicle"]},"segmentation":[{"id":"p1","text":"A driver of a motor vehicle","role":"subject"},{"id":"p2","text":"shall: Exercise due care","role":"standard"},{"id":"p3","text":"to avoid a collision with a pedestrian.","role":"obligation"}],"elements":[{"span":"p1","atoms":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-130::motor_vehicle"],"derived":[],"justification":"'motor vehicle' is admitted as DEF-NRS-484A-130::motor_vehicle, the definition that applies: 'every vehicle which is self-propelled but not operated upon rails', which 'does not include an electric bicycle or an electric scooter'; the run's reading classifies the classes under that id, so the aggregate is claimed whole and never expanded into class atoms. 'A driver of' names who controls the vehicle, no class: it is read through the vehicle's controlled motion (narrowing, observable_reading agency)."},{"span":"p3","atoms":["rel.contact","ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian"],"derived":[],"justification":"'a collision' is rel.contact: 'the bodies of two road users ... touch or overlap (a collision)'. Its exclusion 'Not a stand-in for: a near miss' does not apply: the collision itself is what the duty is to avoid, and the approach short of contact is read by the operationalization of 'due care', not by this atom. 'a pedestrian' is admitted as DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian: 'a person afoot, a person in a manual or motorized wheelchair, or a person on an electric personal assistive mobility device'; claimed whole and read for every road user of the scene other than ego."}],"operationalizations":[{"span":"p2","standard":"due care","concepts":["prim.closest_approach_within","prim.closest_approach_distance","drv.motion.at_rest"],"params":["p.conflict_horizon","p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance","p.rest_speed_tolerance"],"convention":"Traffic-conflict technique: while the motor vehicle moves (drv.motion.at_rest under p.rest_speed_tolerance does not hold), a pedestrian whose centre it approaches within the time-to-collision horizon p.conflict_horizon (prim.closest_approach_within) to a centre distance below p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance (prim.closest_approach_distance) is a conflict the driver's care did not avoid; the projected distance enters the bound itself, so a nearer approach is a graver one. Care is a state of mind and the standard is the whole duty: the rule is a surrogate.","surrogate":true}],"assumptions":[],"intentions":[],"compositions":[],"residues":[],"binders":[{"name":"ego","class_elements":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-130::motor_vehicle"]}],"applicability":"ego is a motor vehicle (ru.aggregate DEF-NRS-484A-130::motor_vehicle). The duty is judged at every observation.","obligation":"At every observation, for every road user p of the scene other than ego that is a pedestrian (ru.aggregate DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian): either ego is at rest (drv.motion.at_rest with p.rest_speed_tolerance), or ego and p are not in contact (rel.contact) and it is not the case that their centres approach within p.conflict_horizon (prim.closest_approach_within) with a centre distance at that closest approach below p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance (prim.closest_approach_distance). With no pedestrian in the scene the duty is met.","params":["p.conflict_horizon","p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance","p.rest_speed_tolerance"],"narrowings":[{"span":"p1","why":"'A driver' is read through the motor vehicle's controlled motion: the duty binds the vehicle's conduct whoever or whatever controls it.","kind":"observable_reading","category":"agency"},{"span":"p2","why":"'due care' is read only as the avoidance of contact and of a projected close approach to a pedestrian while the vehicle moves; lookout, warning and attention are read only through that outcome, and contact made while the vehicle is at rest is not read as a want of care.","kind":"reading"}],"scenarios":[{"name":"forbid","expected":"violated","narrative":"ego, a passenger car (ru.motor_vehicle), drives along a roadway toward a pedestrian crossing in front of it; the centres are projected to approach within the horizon to a distance below the pedestrian clearance, and ego keeps moving."},{"name":"permit","expected":"compliant","narrative":"ego drives past a pedestrian walking on the sidewalk well clear of the roadway; no projected approach falls below the clearance within the horizon and there is no contact."},{"name":"idle","expected":"not_applicable","narrative":"ego is a bicycle (ru.bicycle), which the motor vehicle aggregate does not include; it rides close past a pedestrian."},{"name":"blind","expected":"unknown","narrative":"as in permit, the projected approach stays above the clearance, but the source does not observe contact between bodies, so whether ego touches the pedestrian it passes is unknown.","masked":["rel.contact"]},{"name":"branch_1","expected":"compliant","narrative":"ego drives along an empty road with no pedestrian anywhere in the scene: the duty binds ego alone and is met."}],"literals":[],"param_proposals":[{"name":"p.conflict_horizon","unit":"s","value":3.0,"convention":"Time-to-collision horizon of the traffic-conflict technique: a projected approach of the centres within this time counts toward the danger of a crash.","source":"traffic-conflict technique (time-to-collision thresholds); proposed by NRS_484B_113_3 this iteration"},{"name":"p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance","unit":"m","value":2.5,"convention":"Centre-to-centre distance at the projected closest approach below which the body of a passenger vehicle and a pedestrian would touch or nearly touch: about a design passenger car's half-length plus a pedestrian's body radius.","source":"design passenger car dimensions (AASHTO design vehicle P) and pedestrian body ellipse (HCM)"},{"name":"p.rest_speed_tolerance","unit":"m/s","value":0.1,"convention":"Stop-detection noise floor: the speed at or below which a sampled speed counts as no movement, set at the resolution of the speed measurement so that a complete cessation from movement is recognized despite sensor jitter.","source":"speed-sensor resolution; proposed by LVMC_11_30_030 this iteration"}],"gaps":[],"gap_resolutions":[]}
{"node_id":"NRS_484B_297_6_o1","law_refs":["NRS-484B-297-6"],"subject":{"binder":"ego","class_elements":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian"]},"segmentation":[{"id":"s1","text":"A pedestrian","role":"subject"},{"id":"s2","text":"walking or otherwise traveling on a sidewalk","role":"trigger"},{"id":"s3","text":"who encounters an obstruction to his or her mobility on the sidewalk,","role":"trigger"},{"id":"s4","text":"including, without limitation, a short section of the sidewalk that is missing or impassable,","role":"trigger"},{"id":"s5","text":"may proceed","role":"obligation"},{"id":"s6","text":"with due care","role":"standard"},{"id":"s7","text":"on the immediately adjacent highway","role":"trigger"},{"id":"s8","text":"to move around such an obstruction.","role":"condition"},{"id":"s9","text":"Such a pedestrian:","role":"subject"},{"id":"s10","text":"(a) Must walk or otherwise travel as far to the side of the highway near the sidewalk","role":"sibling"},{"id":"s11","text":"as possible;","role":"sibling"},{"id":"s12","text":"(b) May walk or otherwise travel on the highway in the direction he or she was walking or traveling on the sidewalk, regardless of the direction of traffic;","role":"exception"},{"id":"s13","text":"(c) May walk or otherwise travel in a lane provided for bicycles, electric bicycles or electric scooters","role":"sibling"},{"id":"s14","text":"if the area between the lane and the sidewalk is impassable;","role":"sibling"},{"id":"s15","text":"and","role":"boilerplate"},{"id":"s16","text":"(d) Must return to the sidewalk","role":"sibling"},{"id":"s17","text":"as soon as practicable.","role":"sibling"}],"elements":[{"span":"s1","atoms":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian"],"derived":[],"justification":"'pedestrian' is admitted as DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian: 'a person afoot, a person in a manual or motorized wheelchair, or a person on an electric personal assistive mobility device'; claimed whole."},{"span":"s2","atoms":["rel.occupies","st.sidewalk"],"derived":[],"justification":"st.sidewalk is the 'area for pedestrians between roadway and property line', which carries DEF-NRS-484A-240 ('that portion of a highway between the curb lines or the lateral lines of a highway and the adjacent property lines intended for the use of pedestrians'); ego's footprint overlapping it (rel.occupies) is its walking on it, read at the earlier observation at which ego met the obstruction. 'Not a stand-in for: a crosswalk' does not apply: no crossing is read."},{"span":"s3","atoms":["st.obstruction","st.region_geometry"],"derived":["drv.position.near_region"],"justification":"st.obstruction is 'a physical object on or beside the roadway that is not a road user', kinds including sidewalk_fixture, barrier, excavation, debris: an object in the pedestrian's way on the sidewalk. 'encounters' is ego at the obstruction while on the sidewalk: drv.position.near_region(ego, o) with the reach p.obstruction_reach, over the body-to-region distance of st.region_geometry, the anchor of the obstruction to ego. 'to his or her mobility' is read through ego's conduct at it (s8): it leaves the sidewalk there."},{"span":"s4","atoms":["st.obstruction","st.sidewalk"],"derived":[],"justification":"Nearest atoms only, to show the shortfall (gap on s4): a short section of the sidewalk that is missing is no 'physical object' (st.obstruction), and the absence of a sidewalk region (st.sidewalk) is not a stretch a source records that a pedestrian meets. The example widens where the permission and the duties arise; dropping it would leave the rule unapplied where the law applies."},{"span":"s5","atoms":["rel.occupies","st.travel_region"],"derived":["drv.motion.at_rest"],"justification":"'may proceed' is ego's travel off the sidewalk on the travelled part of the way, the conduct the care qualifies: its footprint overlaps (rel.occupies) a region of kind st.travel_region, the abstract union of 'a lane ..., a bicycle lane or path, a roadway ..., a shoulder, or an intersection', which excludes 'a sidewalk'; it proceeds where it is not at rest (drv.motion.at_rest)."},{"span":"s7","atoms":["rel.occupies","st.highway","st.on_way","st.travel_region","st.sidewalk"],"derived":[],"justification":"'the immediately adjacent highway' is the same way as the sidewalk: the travel region ego occupies and the sidewalk it left both lie on one highway region (st.on_way, 'the public way (a highway region) a structure region lies on'; st.highway, 'the entire public way between its boundary lines, including sidewalks'). Its exclusion 'Not a stand-in for: nearness to a way' is respected: belonging is read through st.on_way, never a distance."},{"span":"s8","atoms":["rel.occupies","st.sidewalk","st.obstruction"],"derived":["drv.position.near_region"],"justification":"'to move around such an obstruction' is a purpose, read through the conduct it explains (observable_reading purpose): since the last observation at which ego occupied the sidewalk while at that obstruction (drv.position.near_region), ego has occupied no sidewalk. 'such an obstruction' is the one s3 binds, re-bound with the same relations."},{"span":"s9","atoms":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian"],"derived":[],"justification":"'Such a pedestrian' is the pedestrian of s1 who proceeds on the highway under the first sentence: the same binder, the same admitted aggregate."}],"operationalizations":[{"span":"s6","standard":"due care","concepts":["prim.closest_approach_within","prim.closest_approach_distance","drv.motion.at_rest","ru.pedestrian"],"params":["p.conflict_horizon","p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance","p.rest_speed_tolerance"],"convention":"Traffic-conflict technique: while the pedestrian moves on the highway (drv.motion.at_rest under p.rest_speed_tolerance does not hold), a road user that is not a pedestrian (ru.pedestrian does not hold) whose centre it approaches within p.conflict_horizon (prim.closest_approach_within) to a centre distance below p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance (prim.closest_approach_distance) is a conflict the pedestrian's care did not avoid; the projected distance enters the bound. Care is a state of mind: the rule is a surrogate.","surrogate":true}],"assumptions":[],"intentions":[],"compositions":[],"residues":[{"span":"s12","why":"A permission to walk in either direction on the highway: no duty of this node turns on the direction of travel, so the duty is the same whether or not its words hold.","scope":"none"}],"binders":[{"name":"ego","class_elements":["ru.aggregate=DEF-NRS-484A-165::pedestrian"]}],"applicability":"ego is a pedestrian. At the judged observation ego occupies no sidewalk and occupies a travel region x (st.travel_region) lying on a highway h (st.on_way) on which a sidewalk s also lies; and, since the last observation at which ego occupied s while at an obstruction o (drv.position.near_region with p.obstruction_reach), ego has occupied no sidewalk at any observation (a since operator). The case of a missing section of sidewalk is not carried (gap on s4).","obligation":"At the judged observation: ego is at rest (drv.motion.at_rest with p.rest_speed_tolerance), or for every road user u other than ego that is not a pedestrian (ru.pedestrian), it is not the case that their centres approach within p.conflict_horizon (prim.closest_approach_within) with a centre distance at that closest approach below p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance (prim.closest_approach_distance). With no such road user in the scene the duty is met.","params":["p.conflict_horizon","p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance","p.rest_speed_tolerance","p.obstruction_reach"],"narrowings":[{"span":"s8","why":"The purpose of moving around the obstruction is read through the conduct it explains: the pedestrian left the sidewalk at the obstruction and has not yet returned.","kind":"observable_reading","category":"purpose"},{"span":"s6","why":"'due care' is read only as the avoidance of a projected close approach to a road user other than a pedestrian while the pedestrian moves on the highway.","kind":"reading"}],"scenarios":[{"name":"forbid","expected":"violated","narrative":"ego walks along the sidewalk, reaches a barrier (st.obstruction) across it, steps onto the adjacent roadway of the same highway and walks into the path of an approaching car; their projected closest approach falls within the horizon below the clearance while ego keeps moving."},{"name":"permit","expected":"compliant","narrative":"as in forbid, but ego steps onto the roadway only after the car has passed; no projected approach to any vehicle falls below the clearance."},{"name":"idle","expected":"not_applicable","narrative":"ego walks along the sidewalk past a mail drop box and never leaves the sidewalk."},{"name":"blind","expected":"unknown","narrative":"ego walks in the roadway beside the sidewalk with a car closing on it, but the source does not observe obstructions, so whether ego left the sidewalk at an obstruction is unknown.","masked":["st.obstruction"]},{"name":"branch_1","expected":"compliant","narrative":"ego moves around a sidewalk excavation on the roadway with no other road user in the scene: the duty binds ego alone and is met."}],"literals":[],"param_proposals":[{"name":"p.conflict_horizon","unit":"s","value":3.0,"convention":"Time-to-collision horizon of the traffic-conflict technique: a projected approach of the centres within this time counts toward the danger of a crash.","source":"traffic-conflict technique (time-to-collision thresholds); proposed by NRS_484B_113_3 this iteration"},{"name":"p.pedestrian_conflict_clearance","unit":"m","value":2.5,"convention":"Centre-to-centre distance at the projected closest approach below which the body of a passenger vehicle and a pedestrian would touch or nearly touch: about a design passenger car's half-length plus a pedestrian's body radius.","source":"design passenger car dimensions (AASHTO design vehicle P) and pedestrian body ellipse (HCM)"},{"name":"p.rest_speed_tolerance","unit":"m/s","value":0.1,"convention":"Stop-detection noise floor: the speed at or below which a sampled speed counts as no movement, set at the resolution of the speed measurement so that a complete cessation from movement is recognized despite sensor jitter.","source":"speed-sensor resolution; proposed by LVMC_11_30_030 this iteration"},{"name":"p.obstruction_reach","unit":"m","value":2.0,"convention":"Reach within which a pedestrian counts as at an obstruction: a walking pedestrian's body width plus one step.","source":"pedestrian body ellipse and walking step length (HCM pedestrian space)"}],"gaps":[{"span":"s4","kind":"atom","proposal":"`st.sidewalk_interruption` | a stretch along a sidewalk where no walkable sidewalk exists (a section missing, removed, closed or broken up), carried as a region with a reference point and a boundary, so a pedestrian's arrival at it is read through st.region_geometry **Not a stand-in for:** an object standing on the sidewalk (st.obstruction) | general","nearest":["st.obstruction: 'a physical object on or beside the roadway that is not a road user' (a missing section of sidewalk is no object, so the atom leaves out where the permission and the duties arise)","st.sidewalk: 'area for pedestrians between roadway and property line' (the absence of a sidewalk region is not a stretch the source records that a pedestrian meets)"]}],"gap_resolutions":[]}
